tives in  supporting at-home learning through  built-in instruction and an emphasis on  computing practices  so we asked does built-in instruction  and a focus on decomposition and  abstraction affect the degree of system  understanding among users and the  quality of code artifacts that they  produce  does experimenter instruction and  question answering in session two impact  the system understanding and code  quality  and how engaging do children find these  two systems  again for today i'm just gonna go over  two of these so we'll talk about system  understanding and code quality  so we looked at two quantitative metrics  and two qualitative metrics to answer  these questions  first we evaluate the degree of  understanding of key aspects of the  system  scored from a rubric based on a post  session quiz  we also quantified the quality of the  code artifacts that children produced  based on a rubric for scoring scratch  junior projects this rubric was pulled  from prior work then we created a direct  mapping adaptation of that rubric to  score the visual story photo projects  we collected qualitative data from  experimenter observations during  gameplay  and from an artifact-based interview at  the end of each session in which we  asked children about the projects that  they'd made  so looking at our data we see an  unsurprising significant effect of  system understanding by session  so as you see here we see an increase in  system understanding in the second  session  after children have had an opportunity  to familiarize themselves with the app  after they've received instruction and  after they've had a chance to ask  questions  and i'll note that here and on all  graphs in this presentation error bars  are 95 confidence intervals  interestingly though we also see a  significant interaction between app and  session  children using scratch junior had a much  larger increase in understanding from  session one to session two  whereas this was not so much the case