hich also means that these concepts  weren't present in their projects in  this first session  so as a follow-up we look only to  session two when students have  familiarized themselves with the app and  what it can do  and we find that students using visual  story coder do create statistically  higher quality stories  we attribute some of this difference to  the app scaffolding around planning  children using visual story coder talked  about the need to plan their stories  whereas those using scratch jr often  mentioned making up their story as they  went along  when asked how they got their idea for  their project one six-year-old using  storyteller said  i got started by thinking who was in the  book and then i was like thinking about  what they would do and it was important  for me to have a plan  on the other hand one seven-year-old  using scratch jr said  i went to like the plus button and i saw  a really pretty flower and i wanted it  to be part of my story so then i thought  of that but i technically made it up as  i went  differences in this reported planning  behavior provide evidence for how visual  story coder can support children's  planning processes  so overall with visual story coder we  demonstrated how multi-modal interfaces  with built-in instruction  help learners better apply computational  thinking practices  shifting gears now to look to the domain  of ai education we first ask how young  learners intuitively think about  conversational ai like the kind they  interacted with in story coder  so when we were developing story coder  we time and again heard stories from  parents about their children's  interactions with conversational ai  there was a four-year-old who when told  his entire family was going on vacation  asked is google coming to  and there was a three-year-old whose  family had a korean rice cooker and  every time the rice was ready it would  say in korean the rice is ready and this  three-year-old would go running over to  the rice cooker to talk to her grandma  the only korean speaker in her life  so with virtual assistants everywhere we  begin to question when a young child  begins to interact with this kind of  technology  what is it they think they're talking to  and how do they extend their cognitive  capacities for reasoning about other  human minds in order to reason about  this kind of ai  so we know that humans have robust  systems for identifying living and  non-living for distinguishing between  agents and objects  and for reasoning about the minds of  other humans  but conversational ai falls into this  unusual psychological pocket of  exhibiting many of the key behaviors of  living agents and humans in particular  while being distinctly non-living  objects  by preschool children's speech is  coherent enough for them to begin  interacting with conversational ai  at this point children have a robust  understanding that agents are goal  directed and have internal responses to  external behavior  they're also beginning to understand  that agents can have knowledge separate  from themselves  and beliefs that differ from ground  truth which both influence this  goal-directed behavior  and further prior work has shown how  children extend this reasoning to  non-human agents  but it remains unclear  what of this intuitive psychology  might they leverage in interactions with  conversational ai  and how might they use these cognitive  capacities to build mental models around  this kind of ai more broadly  limited prior work exists in this space  but in one study  children aged three to six interacted  with google home for a brief period  before being asked to draw google  notably before age six  some of those drawings contained a  human-like feature  like eyes or mouth  here we see that one child said google  is a girl but she doesn't have any arms  after age six though none of those  drawings contained any human-like  elements  so we initially piloted a similar task  and we found similar trends  but the study doesn't ultimately help us  understand how children represent the  mental states of these devices to reason  about their behavior  so we ask how do children reason about  the minds of virtual assistants  one possibility is that children reason  about these devices in the same way that  they'd reason about human minds before  they be begin to distinguish the nuances  of these devices from the minds of  people  so let's look at how children reason  about the minds of people  this paradigm in particular rests  heavily on something called false belief  so let me first explain that  as an example  this is rachel rachel brought raisins to  have during snack time  rachel's in a room with two containers  and rachel decides to put her raisins in  the blue container  then rachel goes away  now while rachel is away joe comes in  joe decides to play a trick on rachel  joe takes rachel's raisins out of the  blue container and moves them to the  green container  and then  joe goes away  so now  rachel comes back in  where will rachel look for her raisins  the blue container or the green  container  hopefully you all realize that in this  scenario joe  knows that the raisins are in the green  container but rachel  falsely believes the raisins are in the  blue container  adults would understand that these two  people have separate minds with separate  information  adults would answer blue container  your average three-year-old though would  answer green container  because three-year-olds typically fail  to represent others false beliefs  this comprehension of false belief is a  key marker in children's understanding  of others minds that we build upon in  our study  so what does the developmental  trajectory of false belief understanding  look like  something like this  three-year-olds typically fail false  belief tasks  they give responses consistent with the  understanding that rachel and joe share  a mind  they would say that rachel would look  for her raisins in the green container  in the years following though we see a  sharp increase in understanding  such that children demonstrate adult  level performance on these false belief  tasks by age six  six year olds would give answers  consistent with the understanding that  people have separate minds  and they would say that rachel would  look for her raisins in the blue  container  so returning to our question we asked  how do children reason about the minds  of virtual assistants  or more specifically how do children  extend their false belief reasoning to  reason about virtual assistants  so we present to children with a false  belief task  so here  scotty and the dark blue shirt goes into  a room and tells one person that his  favorite song is the itsy bitsy spider  then he goes into a different room and  tells a different person that actually  his favorite song is the wheels on the  bus  and finally he returns to the first room  and asks that first person can you  please play my favorite song  our key question is what song will this  person play  hopefully you all say this person will  play the itsy bitsy spider the first  song because that's what scotty told  this first person in this first room was  his favorite song even though he later  corrected that information for a  different agent  your average three-year-old though would  say that this person would play the  wheels on the bus because of their lack  of understanding of false beliefs  now  imagine that scotty was talking to smart  speakers instead  the interaction is the same  what song would the first speaker play  the answer maybe isn't as clear  but if these two devices are in the same  household and connected to the same  account you might infer that the first  smart speaker would actually have the  updated information given to the second  smart speaker and play the second song  the wheels on the bus  turns out adults understand this so here  we ran the study online with 59 adults  on prolific and we plot the proportion  of adults in each condition answering  with each song  in our person condition in gray adults  say that the person in the first room  will play the itsy bitsy spider the  first song  but in the a.i condition in blue  we see that adults say that the smart  speaker in that first room will play the  second song the wheels on the bus  a replication of the study yields the  same results  adult responses and the person condition  are consistent with the understanding  that people have separate minds  but in the ai condition responses are  consistent with the understanding that  smart speakers have a shared mind or a  connected back end  so we've also run 135 children age 3 to  8 through this task conducted remotely  over zoom  as a reminder i've plotted adult level  performance as points here on the right  again the person condition is gray and  the ai condition is blue  so what do children do  looking first to the person condition we  see that our data follows the false  belief trajectory that i previously  showed  three-year-olds are answering  incorrectly on this task because of  their failure to reason about false  beliefs but by age six children are  demonstrating adult level performance  and giving responses consistent with the  understanding that people have separate  minds  turning to the ai condition we might  expect the curve to look something like  this green curve that i've drawn on the  graph  children might start in a similar place  with a lack of representation of false  beliefs  the curve might be bend upward as  children begin to understand false  belief  but it should ultimately end up down at  the bottom somewhere near adult level  understanding  looking at our data though we see that  in the ai condition if we first look at  children who do not have connected smart  speakers at home  their responses again follow the false  belief trajectory  children in this group have not begun to  differentiate the mental states of these  devices from those of people  perhaps because they've not had enough  experience with these devices to start  building separate mental models  but if we look to children that do have  connected smart speakers at home  we see that even children with extensive  experience with these devices  don't give answers that anywhere  resembles adult level understanding  so this is an example of ai that these  children do interact with  but we undoubtedly see a clear  difference in understanding between even  eight-year-olds and adult-level  performance  so returning once more to our research  questions we find that children's  reasoning about the mental states of  these devices follows in line with their  reasoning about humans at least through  age 8 in this false belief task  experience with connected smart speakers  at home may have some impact on this  understanding  but adult level understanding doesn't  emerge until sometime between age 9 and  18.  therefore this work demonstrates that  children's misunderstandings of ai  systems  suggests a need for ai education  that helps to facilitate more accurate  mental model development  which leads me to the last project that  i'll discuss today an accessible system  for ai education  and i do want to mention here that this  next section contains work i did while  working at apple  so conversational ai is just one example  of the kinds of systems ai driven  systems that kids might interact with  with the increasing prevalence of ai and  machine learning in everyday life  we see that researchers and educators  have started advocating  for studying ai machine learning  education as a complement to  contemporary computing education  today robotics is one of the main entry  points to computing but while  state-of-the-art robotics makes heavy  use of ai and machine learning k-12  robotics education today centers on  mechanical engineering and imperative  programming  this makes robotics an ideal context for  introducing ai and machine learning to  students  in particular robotics is a natural fit  for exploring reinforcement learning  which is a type of machine learning used  in state-of-the-art robotics research  but reinforcement learning also drives  some of today's day-to-day ai  technologies that young children might  be interacting with  reinforcement learning has been less  studied in the ai education space but we  also hypothesize it's conceptually easy  comparatively to understand  although robotics is relatively  widespread the cost of specialized  hardware can be a huge barrier to access  so we also aim to create a more  accessible robotics education by  leveraging virtual robots that run on  general purpose hardware tablets and  then bringing these robots to life using  augmented reality  so in our work we built autonomous a  robotics education experience for middle  school students that addresses cost  barriers to wider implementation by  leveraging general purpose hardware  and tests our conjecture that young  learners can develop a conceptual  understanding of reinforcement learning  to make sure we're all on the same page  i'll go over the concept behind  reinforcement learning  reinforcement learning is a machine  learning training method based on  rewarding desired behaviors and  penalizing undesired ones or in other  words it's learning from rewards and  penalties  imagine training a dog  if a dog does something good you might  give it a reward like a treat  it does something bad you might get a  give it a penalty like scolding it  over time that dog might become more or  less likely to take a similar action in  a similar situation  in the context of robotics we can train  a robot robot to navigate using  simulations one that does something good  we can give it points  when it does something bad we'll take  away points  in the autonomous app students use  reinforcement learning to train  autonomous navigation models that  integrate with event driven code  allowing the user to customize their  robot's behavior  users are able to create tracks for  their robots  train virtual robot agents to navigate  these tracks using reinforcement  learning  view simulations and visualizations of  that training  and combine their models with code for  flexible closed-loop robotics  so for the purposes of a demo let's  imagine that the student had this  objective  they wanted a school bus robot to  navigate autonomously around a bus route  track  it should stop flash its lights and load  passengers at every one of the red bus  stops  and it should stop unload all its  passengers again flashing its lights at  the yellow school bus stop at the bottom  so our user here has already recreated  this bus route track in the app  and they start with a partially  pre-trained robot  that means that if we test this current  robot on the bus route track we'll see  that it knows it should be moving  forward but it's not very good at  following the track yet especially on  tight turns  going back we can create a track to  train our robot on the target's an issue  that we've identified so the square  track gives the robot practice on those  tight turns  so we save the track and kick off that  training  training can take some time so speaking  speeding it up a bit here we see that  the car does get better over time  and on episode 50 it actually does  pretty good  so next we can bring this experience to  life and check out a simulation of that  50th episode in augmented reality  finally returning to our bus route track  for another test run now that we've done  some training we see that our robot does  better  but we also see that it still has some  training to do  once students have a working model they  can switch over to the code editor which  will load up the course and the robot  navigation model on the right  and present users with the development  environment on the left  this interface contains a function  library that students can use to  customize robot behavior during event  driven callbacks  for example they could change the  robot's color to yellow at the start of  the run or flash lights and pick up a  passenger at a pickup point  we evaluated this experience in  90-minute remote sessions with 15 middle  school students age 11 to 14.  six of these students were female  9 were male and all of these students  had prior experience with robotics or  hardware  after an introductory phase students  trained a robot to navigate and wrote  code to achieve that school bus  objective that i showed in the demo  we then concluded with about 10 minutes  of questions and feedback  so we measured participants developing  understanding of reinforcement learning  their engagement with the apps and their  curiosity to learn more about machine  learning  across several measures we found that  participants developed an understanding  of reinforcement learning  for example when asked what  reinforcement learning is  93 percent of participants described  reinforcement learning as an agent  learning from rewards penalties or  experiences for future novel situations  for example one 12-year-old said about  reinforcement learning it's when you  have something do something and it takes  your input and it does an action and  then you give it like points or not  points or whatever so that knows when it  did something wrong and when it did  something right  so they can try to do the right thing  next time  looking at participants engagement we  took the seven prompts from the giggle  gauge metric which is a validated  self-report engagement metric from prior  work  participants indicated their agreement  with each prompt on a scale from one to  seven and we report a mean engagement  score of 6.17 out of 7 which indicates  relatively high engagement  from a curiosity standpoint  we asked students their level of  agreement with the statement i would  like to learn more about reinforcement  learning  here they report a mean score of 6.47  out of 7 and all children gave a score  of 5 or greater indicating that they all  agreed with this statement  so through this work we show how virtual  robotics platforms introducing  reinforcement learning to young learners  can create authentic and adaptive  learning experiences  and increase access to robotics  education  overall today i've explored how we can  leverage voice interaction to create new  paradigms in computing education that  are appropriate for pre-literate  learners  how we can extend that approach to even  better support the learning of  computational practices  how children begin to think about and  conceptualize this kind of voice-based  ai  and how we can build on top of existing  infrastructures and computing education  to educate students about ai  in doing so i've demonstrated how  cognitively appropriate and readily  accessible computing education tools  support children's engagement and  learning of core ideas in computing  and i'll be happy to take any questions  [Applause]  so for the purposes of creating a  research prototype we're building  something that's kind of robust enough  for 50 participants in user study  to then go on and release that publicly  would require a lot more development  work which  is in process but it hasn't happened  i'll also mention that a lot of the kind  of  back-end speech processing that happens  runs on right now google cloud and we  chose that because that's the system and  software that best understands young  children's voices  but to then release that publicly every  kind of speech text translation we do  and text-to-speech costs money so for  the purpose of our 50 participants it's  like six dollars but if we were to  release it publicly it would be used by  a lot of people potentially it could get  really expensive very quickly  yeah  so one really cool thing in all the  research  of many different modalities  like  and i'm serious both completely in the  ar example did you see any specific  evidence that having that the ar  track was was more was valuable compared  to just having to see the keys  like if there's anything actually they  got out of that and also more generally  what are other modalities you're  interested in exploring for other  computational thinking or learning  experiences that haven't been as  widespread  yeah so  for the purposes of the study that we  ran we weren't able to test the ar  features and that was a deliberate  choice because in order to use those ar  features you have to have a really  up-to-date ipad  but we wanted to make sure that we were  could be more inclusive participants and  we emphasized or focused our recruitment  on that  so to run those studies we  if students had the ipad or the laptop  that could run  our software then that's how we ran the  studies but for students who didn't we  were able to screen share a simulator of  an ipad  and allow them to interact that way  so in the study we ran we actually  weren't able to test the ar features  there's some prior work that shows  increased engagement when children  learners learn and use var but that's  actually one of the future directions  that i'm excited pursuing is kind of how  we can better integrate ar and then  study ar in an educational context  all right can you hear me  yes  great um i've just noticed that a lot of  the  um  what do you call it the voices that were  in the  ai speech software were i guess more  artificial um and maybe have had the  cadence that might not be necessary for  a human  uh do you think the  this generated text of speech may have  impacted you know children's ability to  differentiate on like  whether  this thing was like of a shared mind or  not um because it's  it kind of sounds like it's clearly  um  an ai generated voice  yeah so there's a few different voices  we heard in the presentation today so in  the software apps the story coder apps  that children use those were google's  text-to-speech voice in the first app  which is quite artificial sounding and  then the second app used google's  wavenet voices which actually sound a  lot more realistic for the purpose of  the video i sped it up so you weren't  listening to a very slow agent talk  so in speeding it up it became a lot  more artificial  those wavenet voices are  surprisingly realistic and are the  voices that are on google homes for  instance  so  as to whether those artificial voices  would impact children's understanding  it's quite possible  but  even with that distinction so even if  children are using  or are recognizing that there's a  difference in this voice from a regular  human voice what our data is showing is  that they're not reasoning about these  devices  kind of differently than they do from a  human mind which is actually really  surprising for us  does that answer your question  yeah it does perfect  okay um i think an interesting time  because the class is over we'll uh wrap  here and i think griffin will stay  around for a few minutes and take a few  questions and then the committee's gonna  move to a  private uh meeting so um thank you  griffin and congratulations  [Music]  [Applause]
 

